{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control or rent leveling ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction. Government-subsidized rents may also be exempt from local rent control.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption runs 30 years from completion of construction. Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings, exempt from municipal rent control for 30 years"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly
constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period
of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"State law exempts newly constructed multiple dwellings from otherwise applicable municipal rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium or cooperative form of ownership or to fee simple ownership of units, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are permitted.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee
simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the
conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase requirements","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered at the increased rent. A rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; notice requirements for rent increases apply statewide, and increases must also comply with municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances where they exist.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenancies"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A tenant may complain to a municipal rent control board where the increase is unconscionable or notice was improper; if the court finds the increase unconscionable, the tenant may be evicted for nonpayment unless the increase is paid.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be
unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest
person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential unit only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause proved in court.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units, under written or oral leases; each ground other than nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written notice to the tenant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential dwelling units; written and oral leases"},"exemptions":"Eviction for nonpayment of rent needs no detailed statement of cause, and in some cases a Notice to Cease must precede the Notice to Quit.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a
lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to
prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit law (transfer and withholding penalties)","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing, paying interest on and returning it. Willfully withholding a deposit of a tenant receiving financial assistance carries a $500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense.","key_value":"$500–$2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Security deposits for residential tenancies. The civil penalty applies where the tenant receives financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenancies; penalty limited to tenants receiving state or federal financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Willful withholding from a tenant receiving public assistance: civil penalty of not less than $500 or more than $2,000 per offense, collected by summary proceeding, payable to the State entity that made the deposits. Unlawful use of deposit monies: disorderly person charge, fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on
behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program,
including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil
penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination housing protections","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing because of race, color, national origin, sex, marital status, disability, familial status, source of lawful income and other listed protected classes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all residential rentals except owner-occupied two-family dwellings and rooms in owner-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, i.e. YMCA and age-restricted housing, as to familial status.","penalty":"Monetary damages, an order to rent the unit to the complainant, and fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD),
N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires
equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin,
ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status,
affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability,
nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
